---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Blk from "../components/Blk.astro";
import Note from "../components/Note.astro";
import Scroll from "../components/Scroll.astro";
import SayYourName from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/SayYourName.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
/* THE FORMS ARE NOT KEPT HERE. Every form of a given name, by language, and whether a family archive has met it on a record,
   is kept ONCE, in Record Atlas (data/given-name-families.json, written only through scripts/given_name_variants.py) and
   published as https://recordatlas.org/given-names.json. The Archive Kit's tools/atlas_given_names.py fetched it into
   src/data/given-names.json (`npm run given-names`), with THIS archive's own count laid over it as `seen` (the Atlas's count
   across every archive is `seenAll`). The count is tools/given_names.py's, from the committed people data: the first given
   name of each person who is not living, a number per form and never a name of a person (src/data/given-names-seen.json).
   A form met in a record here that the Atlas lacks fails `npm run check:given-names`; the fix is `given_name_variants.py add`
   and `publish` in the Record Atlas, and never a table on this page. */
import G from "../data/given-names.json";
import S from "../data/given-names-seen.json";

/* the languages of THIS tree, in the order a reader of it meets them; each one's note is the Atlas's own wording */
const WANT = ["english", "latin", "scots", "irish", "french", "norman", "german", "italian", "spanish"];
const langs = WANT.map((k) => G.languages.find((l) => l.key === k)).filter(Boolean).map(({ key, label, note }) => ({ key, label, note }));

/* A form is counted ONCE however many languages list it (Maria is Latin, Italian and Hungarian: one form, three cards), folded without
   case or accents, as the Atlas and the build gate fold. */
const fold = (s) => String(s || "").toLowerCase().normalize("NFD").replace(/[\u0300-\u036f]/g, "").trim();
const byForm = new Map();
for (const f of G.rows.flatMap((r) => Object.values(r.langs || {}).flat())) {
  const k = fold(f.form);
  const prev = byForm.get(k) || { here: 0, all: 0 };
  byForm.set(k, { here: Math.max(prev.here, f.seen || 0), all: Math.max(prev.all, f.seenAll || 0) });
}
const nDistinct = byForm.size;
const attestedHere = [...byForm.values()].filter((f) => f.here > 0).length;
const aidsOnlyElsewhere = [...byForm.values()].filter((f) => !(f.here > 0) && f.all > 0).length;

const obs = Object.entries(S.observed["first given name"]);
const top = [...obs].sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1] || a[0].localeCompare(b[0])).slice(0, 16);
const left = Object.values(S.leftOut).reduce((a, n) => a + n, 0);
const once = obs.filter(([, n]) => n === 1).length;
const nForms = obs.length;
const chips = ["William", "John", "Elizabeth", "Conyers", "Lindesay", "Catherine", "Margaret", "Giuseppina", "Karl", "Constantine"];
---
<Base
  title="The forenames"
  eyebrow={`${nForms} forenames met here · ${S.counted} people counted · ${G.rows.length} names in the Atlas catalogue`}
  dek="One person, written down by clerks in different languages, in different centuries. Type a given name and see every form the same human being could have been entered under, and which of them this archive has met.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">A <strong>Joannes</strong> in a Latin register, a <strong>John</strong> in an English index and a
    <strong>Giovanni</strong> in an Italian civil act are the same name. An index that treats them as three people will never
    join a family up, and neither will a searcher who types only the one the tree happens to hold. This tree runs from Norman
    Yorkshire and the Palatinate to Gloucestershire, Chatham, Brisbane and the Campania of the Falco line, so its forenames
    were written in a good many hands.</p>

    <p><strong>A name you have looked up is a link you can send.</strong> The address bar follows the search
    (<span class="mono">?n=Conyers</span>), so a card can go to a cousin or an archivist and open on exactly what you were looking at.</p>

    <p><strong>Two kinds of cell.</strong> A form printed plainly is one this archive has met in its own tree. A form marked
    <span class="aid">like this<sup>°</sup></span> is a <strong>lookup aid</strong>: the correct form in that language and the
    right thing to type into an index tomorrow, but not something written down here. <strong>{attestedHere} of the
    {nDistinct} forms in the catalogue are met here; {nDistinct - attestedHere} are aids</strong>
    ({aidsOnlyElsewhere} of them another family archive has met, which the Record Atlas keeps). The line under the box counts a form once for every language that lists it (Maria is Latin, Italian and Hungarian), so its figures are larger than these.</p>
  </section>

  <SayYourName rows={G.rows} u={u} langs={langs} chips={chips} />

  <Blk label="Where the forms live" title="Not on this page">
    <p>No table of forms is kept in this archive. Every form of a given name, with the evidence that it was met, is kept once, in the
    <a href="https://recordatlas.org/your-name/">Record Atlas</a>, and this page is read from that catalogue
    (<a href="https://recordatlas.org/given-names.json">given-names.json</a>) when the site is built. Only one thing is this
    archive's own: the count of how often it has met a form. If a form turns up in the tree that the catalogue does not hold, the
    build stops until it is added there, with its evidence.</p>
    <Note>
      <strong>What is counted.</strong> The first given name of each of the {S.notLiving} people this archive publishes as people and who
      are not living ({S.counted} of them readable that way, in {nForms} different forms; {once} of the forms were met once).
      <strong>Living people are neither counted nor named.</strong> A middle name is not counted: a tree writes surnames, saints' names
      and nicknames there as often as names. {left} entries were left out because the tree's name is not a given name and a surname: a
      one-word name, a title with a place, a particle, a placeholder, an initial or a note about a family. These are counts of a
      family tree, which writes some names as it found them on other people's pages, not of registers.
    </Note>
  </Blk>

  <Blk label="In this tree" title="The commonest forenames">
    <p class="dek">The {top.length} forms most often written first in the tree, as a number of people. A form here has a card above:
    type it in the box.</p>
    <Scroll label="The commonest first given names in the tree">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th class="m">People</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {top.map(([f, n]) => (
            <tr><td><a href={u(`/your-name/?n=${encodeURIComponent(f)}`)}>{f}</a></td><td class="m">{n}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Scroll>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:.8rem">A surname does the same thing: see <a href={u("/name/")}>The Name</a> and
    <a href={u("/spellings/")}>Every Spelling of One Name</a>. Where a name is searched, <a href={u("/searched/")}>What Has Been Read</a>
    says which spellings and forms were tried.</p>
  </Blk>
</Base>

<style is:global>
  p .aid { color: var(--ink-3); font-style: italic; }
  p .aid sup { font-style: normal; }
</style>
